variants.practitioner:
TypeScript strict mode is configured in tsconfig.json with all strict flags enabled. This means every function parameter has an explicit type, every return value is type-checked, and null/undefined are handled explicitly. When implementing new features, the compiler will catch type errors before runtime.

The module system is ESM throughout. Import statements use .js extensions (even for TypeScript files — this is required for ESM resolution):
import { StorageProvider } from '../storage/types.js';
import { SessionState } from '../types/index.js';

The frontend in web/index.html uses vanilla JS with fetch() calls to the REST API. All API calls follow the pattern: const response = await fetch('/api/projects'); const projects = await response.json(); No build step, no transpilation, no module bundler. The HTML file is served as-is.

For testing, vitest is the test runner. Test files in tests/unit/ import directly from src/ without pre-compilation. Vitest handles TypeScript natively through its esbuild transform. Test patterns:
import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest';
import { CoreEngine } from '../../src/engine/core.js';

The package.json defines the project as type: 'module' (ESM). All .js files are treated as ESM. No CommonJS (require()) anywhere in the codebase.

For new modules, create files in the appropriate src/ subdirectory (engine/, storage/, llm/, etc.), export the public API through an index.ts barrel file, and add tests in tests/unit/. The build command (tsc) compiles everything to dist/ preserving the directory structure.
